    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`). Here's a summary of the change, which implements owner deletion as a soft-delete.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Domain (`Owner.java`)** \u2014 Added a `deleted` boolean field (mapped to a new `deleted` column) with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted(...)`. A primitive `boolean` defaults to `false`, so newly created owners are not deleted.\n\n**Service (`ClinicServiceImpl.java`)** \u2014 the facade where this logic belongs:\n- `deleteOwner(...)` now flags the owner deleted and saves it (`owner.setDeleted(true); ownerRepository.save(owner)`) instead of removing the row.\n- `findOwnersByIdentityKey(...)` and `findHouseholdDuplicateOf(...)` \u2014 the two create-time duplicate/identity checks \u2014 now skip owners flagged deleted, so a would-be duplicate is allowed when the only match has been soft-deleted. Updated their Javadocs accordingly.\n\n**API contract (`openapi.yml`)** \u2014 Added a `readOnly` boolean `deleted` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and MapStruct maps it automatically (both sides have a `deleted` property).\n\n**Schemas (`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`)** \u2014 Added a `deleted` column defaulting to false/0 with `NOT NULL`, keeping the DEFAULT-before-NOT-NULL ordering HSQLDB requires.\n\n## Behavior now\n- `DELETE /api/owners/{id}` returns 2xx and retains the row with `deleted=true`.\n- `GET /api/owners/{id}` still returns the owner afterward, with `deleted=true`.\n- A newly created owner has `deleted=false`.\n- Re-creating an owner whose only matching record was deleted is allowed.\n\nThe controller (`OwnerRestControllerV1.deleteOwner`) was left untouched \u2014 it already looks up the owner and delegates to the service, which is the right seam for the soft-delete logic.\n\nExisting endpoints that count/scan all owners (city capacity, daily limit, namesakes, customer-code dedup, household size) were intentionally left counting all rows, since the spec scoped the \"ignore deleted\" behavior specifically to the duplicate and identity checks.",
